> But enchanting base ac:1 items first means the AC never drops and at
> least temporarily rises, so you are likewise never more vulnerable
> until you can change armor and you'll be better off if you do change
> armor.
> 
> Suppose you have an orcish helm and some leather gloves, both +0.
> 
> You could rustproof the helm now.  Then your AC is 8 now (1 for
> gloves, 1 for helm) and will remain 8 if you meet a rust monster.
> 
> Or you could enchant the gloves.  Then your AC is _at least_ 7 now (2
> for gloves, 1 for helm), with a 33% chance of being 6 now and a 33%
> chance of being 5 now.  And once you meet a rust monster, it's at
> least 8 still after the helm gets rusted (and equally likely to be 7
> or 6). So it's not worse off even with the rusty helm, and once you
> grab another helm it's back to guaranteed better off.
> 
> In other words, if you enchant your AC will never drop below its
> current 8.  And if you rustproof, your AC will never drop below its
> current 8.  But if you enchant, your AC will be 1-3 points better in
> the interim (before meeting a rust monster), and 66% of the time it
> will also be 1-2 points better even after you meet the rust monster.
> 
> That's what I mean by "with base ac:1 items, your AC is never worse if
> you enchant now".
> 
> The analysis of base ac:2 is a little more complex, but if your armor
> isn't all +3 or better you are likewise definitely better off
> enchanting now instead of rustproofing (worst case you could actually
> be worse than rustproofing at some point, but you're always better now
> and the majority of the time you're still at least as well off later).
> If all your armor is +3 then proofing an ac2 item (most likely
> fireproofing a robe) might make sense depending on how likely damage
> is, etc.
> 
> 

